<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
xml:id="sec-definition-of-continuity-examples">

  <title>Definition of Continuity and Examples</title> <subsection
  xml:id="subsec-continuity-at-a-point">

  <title>Continuity at a Point</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>S \subseteq \R</m> and let <m>f \colon S \to \R</m> be a function.
      We say that <m>f</m> is <term>continuous at</term><idx>continuous at
      a point</idx> <m>c \in S</m> if for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>
      there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that whenever <m>x \in S</m> and
      <m>|x-c| \lt \delta</m>, we have <md>|f(x)-f(c)| \lt \varepsilon.</md>
      In other words, <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>c</m> if the output
      <m>f(x)</m> can be made arbitrarily close to <m>f(c)</m> provided
      that the input <m>x</m> is sufficiently close to <m>c</m>.
    </p> <p>
      If <m>A \subseteq S</m>, then <m>f</m> is <term>continuous
      on</term><idx>continuous on a set</idx> <m>A</m> if it is continuous
      at every point of <m>A</m>. We say simply that <m>f</m> is
      <term>continuous</term><idx>continuous function</idx> if it is
      continuous on its entire domain.
    </p> <p>
      The definition refers only to points <m>x</m> that lie in the domain
      <m>S</m>. Thus, continuity at <m>c</m> concerns the behavior of <m>f</m>
      at domain points near <m>c</m>; we do not need <m>f</m> to be defined
      on an entire open interval around <m>c</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection> <subsection xml:id="subsec-cluster-and-isolated-points">
    <title>Cluster Points and Isolated Points</title> <p>
      A <term>neighborhood</term> of a point <m>c \in \R</m> is a subset
      of <m>\R</m> that contains an open interval around <m>c</m>.  In
      particular, every open interval containing <m>c</m> is a neighborhood
      of <m>c</m>.
    </p> <p>
      Let <m>S \subseteq \R</m>. A real number <m>c</m> is called a <term>cluster
      point</term><idx>cluster point</idx> of <m>S</m> if for every <m>\delta
      \gt 0</m> there exists <m>x \in S</m> such that <m>x \neq c</m> and
      <m>|x-c| \lt \delta</m>. In other words, <m>c</m> is a cluster point of
      <m>S</m> if every neighborhood of <m>c</m> contains a point in <m>S</m>
      other than <m>c</m>. A point <m>c \in S</m> is an <term>isolated
      point</term><idx>isolated point</idx> of <m>S</m> if it is not a
      cluster point of <m>S</m>. That is, there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m>
      such that <md>S \cap (c-\delta, c+\delta)=\{c\}.</md> Equivalently,
      some neighborhood of <m>c</m> contains no points of <m>S</m> other than
      <m>c</m>. A cluster point of <m>S</m> need not belong to <m>S</m>, but
      an isolated point of <m>S</m>, by definition, must belong to <m>S</m>.

    </p> <example xml:id="eg-cluster-and-isolated-points">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
	  <li> The set of cluster points of an open interval <m>(a,b)</m>
	    is the closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
	  </li>

	    <li> The set of isolated points of <m>\Z</m> is <m>\Z</m> itself.
	    In other words, every integer is isolated.

          </li>
            <li> In the set <m>\{0\} \cup \{1/n : n \in \N\}</m>, the point
            <m>0</m> is a cluster point, while each point <m>1/n</m> is an
            isolated point.
          </li> </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-isolated-points-are-continuity-points">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A function is continuous at every isolated point of its domain.
        </p>
      </statement> <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>c</m> be an isolated point of the domain <m>S</m> of a
          function <m>f</m>. Then there exists <m>\delta_0 \gt 0</m>
          such that <m>S \cap (c-\delta_0,c+\delta_0)=\{c\}</m>. Thus, whenever
          <m>x \in S</m> and <m>|x-c| \lt \delta_0</m>, necessarily <m>x=c</m>.
          Hence <md>|f(x)-f(c)|=|f(c)-f(c)|=0</md> which is less than any
          positive <m>\varepsilon</m>. This shows that <m>f</m> is continuous
          at <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition> <p>
        A cluster point is also called a <term>limit point</term><idx>limit
	point</idx>. The following proposition explains this terminology.
    </p> <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A real number <m>c</m> is a cluster point of <m>S \subseteq \R</m>
          if and only if there is an injective sequence <m>(x_n)</m> in
          <m>S</m> that converges to <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </statement> <proof>
        <p>
          First suppose that <m>c</m> is a cluster point of <m>S</m>. We
          construct an injective sequence <m>(x_n)</m> in <m>S</m> such that
          <m>x_n \to c</m>. Since <m>c</m> is a cluster point, there exists
          <m>x_1 \in S</m> such that <m>0 \lt |x_1-c| \lt 1</m>. Now suppose
          that <m>x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}</m> have been chosen in <m>S</m>, all
          distinct, with <m>0 \lt |x_k-c| \lt 1/k</m> for <m>k=1,\dots,n-1</m>.
          Let <md>\eta=\min\{|x_1-c|,\dots,|x_{n-1}-c|\} \gt 0.</md> Because
          <m>c</m> is a cluster point of <m>S</m>, there exists <m>x_n \in
          S</m> such that <md>0 \lt |x_n-c| \lt \min\{1/n,\eta\}.</md> Then
          <m>x_n \neq x_k</m> for <m>k=1,\dots,n-1</m>, because <m>|x_n-c|
          \lt |x_k-c|</m>. Thus, by induction, we obtain an injective sequence
          <m>(x_n)</m> in <m>S</m> with <m>|x_n-c| \lt 1/n</m> for every
          <m>n</m>. Since <m>1/n \to 0</m>, it follows that <m>|x_n-c| \to
          0</m>, and hence <m>x_n \to c</m>.
        </p> <p>
	  Conversely, suppose there is an injective sequence <m>(x_n)</m>
	  in <m>S</m> such that <m>x_n \to c</m>. Let <m>\delta \gt 0</m>.
	  Since <m>x_n \to c</m>, <m>|x_n-c| \lt \delta</m> for all <m>n</m>
	  sufficiently large. In particular, the inequality is satisfied
	  by infinitely many terms of the sequence. At most one of them
	  can equal <m>c</m>, by injectivity. Therefore, <m>x_N \neq c</m> for
	  some sufficiently large <m>N</m>, and <m>|x_N-c| \lt \delta</m>
	  as well. This shows that every neighborhood of <m>c</m> contains
	  a point of <m>S</m> other than <m>c</m>. Therefore, <m>c</m> is a
	  cluster point of <m>S</m>.
	</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-examples-of-continuous-functions">
    <title>Examples of Continuous Functions</title> <p>
      The simplest examples of continuous functions are the constant
      functions and the identity function <m>\mathrm{id}(x)=x</m>. We
      leave the justification of these statements as an exercise.
    </p>

      <proposition xml:id="prop-mx-continuous">
      <statement>
      <p>
         For any <m>m \in \R</m>, the function <m>x \mapsto mx</m>
	 is continuous.
      </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
	Fix <m>x_0 \in \R</m>. Given <m>\ve \gt 0</m>, choose
	<m>\delta = \ve/(1+|m|)</m>. Then <m>\delta \gt 0</m>, and whenever
	<m>|x-x_0| \lt \delta</m>, <md number="no"> |mx-mx_0| = |m||x-x_0|
	\lt (1+|m|)\delta = \ve.  </md> </p>
      </proof> </proposition>

    <exercise xml:id="prop-absolute-value-continuous">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The absolute value function is continuous on <m>\R</m>.
        </p>
      </statement> <hint> <p>The preceding proposition implies that the absolute
      value function is continuous at every nonzero real number. It remains
      to show that it is also continuous at <m>x=0</m>. </p> </hint>
    </exercise>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-reciprocal-continuous">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The reciprocal function <m>f(x)=1/x</m> is continuous on <m>\R
          \setminus \{0\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement> <proof>
        <p>
	Fix <m>x_0 \neq 0</m>. Since <m>x_0^2 \gt 0</m>, the
	continuity of the function <m>x \mapsto x_0x</m> (<xref
	ref="prop-mx-continuous"/>) gives <m>\delta \gt 0</m>
	such that <md number="no"> -\frac{x_0^2}{2} \lt x_0x - x_0^2 \lt
	\frac{x_0^2}{2} </md> In particular, <m>xx_0 \gt x_0^2/2</m>.
	Thus, <md number='no'> \left|\frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{x_0}\right|
	= \frac{|x-x_0|}{|xx_0|} \lt \frac{2}{x_0^2}|x-x_0| </md> whenever
	<m>|x-x_0| \lt \delta</m>. From this we conclude that <m>1/x</m> is
	continuous at <m>x_0</m>, since <m>|x-x_0| \to 0</m> as
	<m>x\to x_0</m>. Finally, since <m>x_0 \neq 0</m> is arbitrary, we
	conclude that <m>x \mapsto 1/x</m> is continuous.
        </p>
      </proof>

    </proposition> <example xml:id="eg-continuous-functions-on-discrete-domains">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the domain consists entirely of isolated points, then every
          function on that domain is continuous. For example, every function
          <m>f \colon \Z \to \R</m> is continuous, because each integer is
          an isolated point of <m>\Z</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example> <p>
      Later we will prove that sums, products, quotients (where the denominator
      is nonzero), and compositions of continuous functions are again
      continuous. From those general results it will follow that every
      polynomial is continuous on <m>\R</m>, every rational function is
      continuous on its domain, and standard functions such as <m>\sin
      x</m>, <m>\cos x</m>, <m>e^x</m>, and <m>\ln x</m> are continuous
      on their natural domains.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
